    'Levels of acne severity',
    ['<b>Mild</b>: mostly whiteheads and blackheads, with a few red spots or pustules', '<b>Moderate</b>: more red spots and pustules, mostly on the face', '<b>Moderately severe</b>: many red spots and pustules, with occasional inflamed lumps', '<b>Severe</b>: many deep cysts or nodules, with a risk of scarring'],
    'Common causes',
    ['Hormonal changes, especially in the teenage years, before a period or during pregnancy', 'Genetics', 'Some skincare products or cosmetics that clog pores', 'Stress', 'In adults who suddenly develop severe acne for the first time, it can sometimes be linked to another health condition that should be checked'],
    'How to look after your skin',
    ['Wash your face twice a day with a gentle cleanser, without scrubbing hard', 'Choose skincare and cosmetics labelled non-comedogenic (won’t clog pores)', 'Avoid squeezing or picking spots, as this increases the risk of scars', 'Use products containing benzoyl peroxide or azelaic acid, following basic guidance'],
    'When should you see a dermatologist?',
    'If over-the-counter products have not helped within 3–4 months, or your acne is getting worse — for example with deep inflamed lumps or cysts — see a dermatologist for more effective treatment. You should also see a doctor if acne is seriously affecting your mood, such as causing stress or anxiety.',
    'Acne care at HappiWell Clinic',
    f'At {CLINIC} in Thung Khru, a doctor can examine your skin, advise on acne care and consider suitable medicine based on how severe your acne is. Booking is easy, with no long wait.',
    BOOK,
    'Frequently asked questions',
    '<b>Can I squeeze my own spots?</b><br>It is best not to, as it increases the risk of infection and scarring. Let a doctor or skin professional deal with them properly.',
    '<b>Can acne go away on its own without treatment?</b><br>Mild acne sometimes improves by itself, but in most cases the right products help it clear faster and reduce the risk of scars.',
    '<b>Is it normal to get acne for the first time as an adult?</b><br>It can happen, especially due to hormonal changes. But if severe acne appears suddenly in adulthood, talk to a doctor to check for other possible causes.',
    '<b>Do fried food and chocolate really cause acne?</b><br>The link between diet and acne is less clear than the role of hormones and genetics. But if you notice more spots after certain foods, you can try avoiding them.',
